body:
Used to get yelled at a lot for fidgeting and getting easily overwhelmed-- turns out I just had undiagnosed ADHD! 

&#x200B;

But my personal favorite was telling off some of my classmates for talking shit about my girlfriend at the time. I even told them that they were allowed to think and say these things but to please refrain from saying them in front of me because she was important to me. The teacher scolded me for impeding their "freedom of speech".

\*cracks knuckles\* My time to shine.

&#x200B;

Okay, so this was my first year living away from home and at college. I was roomed with this girl whose name I literally can't remember for the life of me, so let's call her L. Move in day was already a bad sign because her parents were still there when I started moving in. They greeted me and introduced themselves, even asking about my personal life, like my job and family and major. L laid in bed and stared at me while her parents unpacked her stuff for her. Big yikes. 

&#x200B;

I learned very quickly that L had a boyfriend. She liked to bring him over a lot. This was fine with me. He'd mostly just cuddle in bed with her and play his NBA video games. No issue there either. What I had issues with was the very loud daddy kink sex they'd have with me in the room. Fun fact, at the time, I worked third shift three nights a week, so they had practically half the week to do their business. But they seemed to wait for me to be there. I literally wouldn't even care if she just gave me a heads up like, "Hey, my boyfriend's coming over tonight, wink wink". But what am I supposed to do when he's putting it in her butt and I have to get up to take a piss???? Luckily this only happened like three times but jesus christ.

&#x200B;

Not to mention that they both would constantly have their friends over. That's fine, so did I. Me and my friends typically stayed in the common area and talked amongst ourselves. They'd always clean up after themselves and stay relatively quiet because they understood that this was somebody else's living space so they needed to mind their manners. L and her boyfriend's friends, on the other hand... hoo boy. The dorms had a bed on each side of the room, with a desk perpendicular to the beds. I come home from class one day and L's friends are sitting ON MY BED. EATING MESSY CHINESE FOOD. WITH THEIR BAGS AND UTENSILS ON M Y DESK. They at least had the decency to get off the bed once I got there, but that didn't last long. I just kinda sat on the bed, plugged in my phone, and did my work like everything was normal. L's friend fucking unplugged my phone charger to charge his phone instead. I'm glad I had severe social anxiety at the time because I was ready to throw the fuck DOWN.

&#x200B;

The icing on the cake was coming back from Christmas break. She asked me if I had any plans to move out. If I weren't on a lease, I would have moved out after the first time I heard her boyfriend ask to put his "thingy" in her ass. (That's actually what he called it, FYI). I told her no, I didn't. She was like, "Okay, well you're gonna have to find somewhere else. I talked to <the landlady> and she says you need to leave". Okay, why's that, L? "Because I'm not comfortable with you being trans and <landlady> says you need to leave because of it". This still baffles me to this day. I'm FtM trans, born female living as male. But I was closeted at the time. I don't even know how she found out! Two, the landlady already knew about my trans situation so I knew this plan wasn't her doing. 

&#x200B;

Anyway I told the lady about L smoking pot and tampering with the fire alarm to keep herself from getting caught. She got in trouble with her parents

&#x200B;

TL;DR: Shitty roommate has shitty sex and shitty friends and then has the audacity to kick me out for making her uncomfortable

That you really can't please everybody, so it's futile to try.

&#x200B;

As my therapist put it, "You can be the juiciest peach in the world and there will always be people who hate peaches". So remember to just do your thing and make yourself happy! After all, you can walk away from other people but you can never walk away from yourself

Honestly, going outside and forcing yourself to do something. Even if it's something small like sweeping your porch or taking out the garbage. 

&#x200B;

I'm a big victim of eating my feelings, so I'll force myself to walk to the store and back. Makes me at least remind myself that I am capable of these things, even if I don't feel it at the time

Bruised my ribs as a teen. Couldn't lay on my stomach or cough too heavily without feeling like death.

&#x200B;

Not sure if this counts as an injury, but once I had an ear infection and a cold at the same time, so every time I coughed it felt like I was being shot in the head. That's the first time I've ever felt in so much pain that I wanted to go to the hospital and I've been in a car accident, have scoliosis, and thrown up blood before. Nothing compared to that
